Theme structure
===============
The OpenOLAT themes follow the bootstrap terminology. 
We also rely heavily on SASS: 
	"Sass is an extension of CSS3, adding nested rules, variables, mixins, selector inheritance, and more. 
	It's translated to well-formatted, standard CSS using the command line tool or a web-framework plugin." 
	(http://sass-lang.com/).

In the default theme ("light"), the SASS (css) rules are splitted up in different many different module files
for better readibility and managebility. 

There are two CSS files which are compiled and used in OpenOLAT: 
- theme.css: this file is included in the OpenOLAT application. It contains all styles for the application.
- content.css: this file is included in learning content pages within iframes. This can be disabled for 
  single pages, scorm or cp files. The content.css contains several classes starting with b_* which can be
  selected in the HTML for simple page styling. 


Sass-Files in the light theme:
------------------------------
Note this is the base theme, normally you do not modify those files but create a new theme that inherits from
the light theme and extends on top of it. 

- theme.scss: defines what to include for the main css file
- content.scss: defines what to include for the content css file used in iframe content
- favicon.ico: favicon that is displayed for the OpenOLAT server in the browser
- theme.js (optional): javascript code that should be embedded to the page to add some more complex
  features that can't be done with CSS alone
- _config.scss: all OpenOLAT and bootstrap SASS variables
- _palette.scss: the colour palette for all user colour choosers (14 hues x 10 steps), the swatch roles
  and the semantic states of the status labels ($o-states)
- _palette_theme.scss: the theme colours, the brand colours of the theme the choosers offer after the 14 hues
- _palette_neutral.scss: the neutral scale, the 18 greys of the theme (--o-neutral-{step})
- modules/*: tons of include files for different aspects of OpenOLAT


Creating a custom theme
=======================

A good way is to start with a copy of "openolat" (i.e. copy openolat to mytheme). 
- Copy from the openolat theme:
  cd openolat/src/main/webapp/static/themes/
  cp -r openolat to mytheme
- Rename mytheme/_openolat_config.scss to mytheme/_mytheme_config.scss
- Rename mytheme/_openolat_theme.scss to mytheme/_mytheme_theme.scss
- Rename mytheme/_openolat_content.scss to mytheme/_mytheme_content.scss
- Rename mytheme/_openolat_email.scss to mytheme/_mytheme_email.scss
- Open mytheme/theme.sass and replace the references to the files you just renamed 
  (openolat_config -> mytheme_config, openolat_theme -> mytheme_theme)
- Open mytheme/content.sass and replace the references to the files you just renamed
  (openolat_config -> mytheme_config, openolat_content -> mytheme_content)
- Open mytheme/email.sass and replace the references to the files you just renamed
  (openolat_config -> mytheme_config, openolat_email -> mytheme_email)
- Open mytheme/_mytheme_config.scss and add all bootstrap and OpenOLAT SASS variables you want to 
  override. As references yo can look at the following files:
  - for all bootstrap variables: ../../bootstrap/stylesheets/bootstrap/_variables.scss
  - for all OpenOLAT variables: light/_config.scss
  - for the chooser colour palette: light/_palette.scss. You can override the values of $o-palette.
    Keep all 14 hue names, the alias tables need them, otherwise the compile stops. Every step needs
    a foreground with a contrast of 4.5:1, otherwise the compile fails. OpenOLAT reads the palette
    from the compiled theme.css of the active theme, so badges and report charts follow your
    palette. A theme without the --o-{hue}-{step} properties in its theme.css uses the palette of
    the light theme, without theme colours.
  - for the theme colours: light/_palette_theme.scss. Set $o-theme-colors to the brand colours of
    the client, default is ($brand-primary). Example with 3 colours:
      $o-theme-colors: ($brand-primary, $brand-accent, #0090C5);
    The compile generates 10 steps per colour; the colour itself is the solid fill when a text
    colour reaches 4.5:1 on it. The choosers show them as "Theme 1" ... "Theme n" after the 14 hues.
    An entry can also be a map with all 10 steps (50: ..., 900: ...). The theme colours need
    Dart Sass 1.79.0 or later.
  - for the greys: light/_palette_neutral.scss. The neutral scale has 18 steps: 0 (white), 25,
    50, 100, 150, 200, 250, 300, 400, 450, 500, 600, 700, 750, 800, 900, 950 and 1000 (black),
    emitted as --o-neutral-{step}. The default greys are tinted with slate. To change the tint,
    set the OKLCH hue and chroma in your config:
      $o-neutral-hue: 30;        // default 260 (slate)
      $o-neutral-chroma: 0;      // default 0.038; 0 gives pure greys
    The compile keeps the lightness of each step, so the contrast on white stays almost the
    same. The choosers do not offer the neutral scale. The modules do not read a step directly:
    they read a semantic or element token, and a token can read a step, e.g.
      $o-tokens: ("text-muted": "neutral-600");
  - for the status colours: light/_palette.scss. Status labels, badges and status icons use 8
    semantic states (neutral, success, warning, critical, danger, finished, inactive, highlight).
    $o-states assigns a palette hue to each state. To change it, set the full map with all 8 states
    in your config, e.g. ("neutral": "blue", "success": "green", ...). Do not style a status class directly: modules/_states.scss wires every status class
    to its state. For a new status use the classes "o_labeled o_state_<name>" (styles o_state_dark,
    o_state_tint, o_state_light) or "o_labeled_light o_state_<name>" (outline). In SCSS use
    o-state-color($state, $role, $style) for a component colour.
  - the colours are CSS custom properties (colour tokens) in three layers:
      base       the palette (--o-{hue}-{step}, the neutral scale --o-neutral-{step}, --o-white
                 and the base colours --o-base-*), the semantic tokens (--o-text, --o-heading,
                 --o-primary, --o-bg-hover, ...), the state tokens
                 (--o-state-{state}-{role}) and the global elements (buttons, content text and
                 headings, e.g. --o-btn-default-fg, --o-content-h1)
      framework  layout and generic UI elements, e.g. --o-navbar-bg, --o-footer-link,
                 --o-tree-link, --o-tag-bg
      component  the elements of one feature module, e.g. --o-coursesite-start-bg,
                 --o-qti-menu-link, --o-selectus-review-glow
    The modules read the tokens with var(--o-*). The base colours come from your Sass variables
    ($text-color, $headings-color, $brand-primary, $link-color, ...), so keep setting them as
    before. The light defaults read three colour scales only: the palette (12 chromatic hues),
    the neutral scale with white and the brand ramp theme1 (OO-10043 slice 25), e.g.
    $panel-default-border: o-neutral(150), $o-base-orange-color: o-palette("orange", 500),
    $o-base-blue-color: o-palette("blue", 600). A default with colour maths on another
    variable keeps its source and gives the nearest scale step of its result
    (o-scale-snap()); when you set the source to your own colour, the result stays the
    computed colour as before. Your own colours stay literal values. Only the brand colour
    itself and the defaults that derive from $brand-primary (button, link, primary tokens, the
    checked radio card) read the brand ramp and follow your $brand-primary; a fixed blue reads
    the palette (blue).
    An element token (base, framework or component) has one of two defaults:
      - a Sass variable of the theme config ($o-navbar-bg, $btn-default-color, $o-bookmark-color,
        ...): keep setting the variable as before, it gives the default of the token. For a link
        or status text on a coloured background (e.g. --o-link-subtle, --o-badge-status-info)
        the default is the text colour that o-a11y-color() adjusts for that background. When you set such a token with $o-tokens, check the contrast yourself;
      - a reference to another token, e.g. --o-selectus-review-glow reads --o-primary and
        --o-qti-menu-link reads --o-tree-link. There is no Sass variable: set the token with
        $o-tokens.
    modules/_tokens.scss lists every token with its default.
    The buttons have shade tokens for the hover, focus and active state, e.g.
    --o-btn-primary-bg-hover and --o-btn-primary-border-focus. The compile computes them as the
    darker colour of --o-btn-primary-bg and --o-btn-primary-border, so they follow your button
    colours. Set a shade token with $o-tokens only when you want a different state colour. When
    you override a button background with $o-tokens, override its border token too. Other
    elements have shade tokens too: the compile computes them with the darken() or lighten()
    percentage of the module from the element token, so your Sass variable gives the same
    colour as before. When the token colour is a scale step (a light default), the shade
    token reads the nearest step of the same hue (neutral scale, brand ramp) that differs
    from the token and keeps the contrast of its text, e.g. --o-btn-primary-bg-hover:
    var(--o-theme1-600). A bright fill at step 400 with dark text (the warning and success
    fills, the dirty button) goes lighter: hover step 300, pressed step 200 of its hue. The name is the token name and a suffix:
      -hover, -active, -focus   the colour of a state, e.g. --o-tag-bg-hover, --o-chelp-bg-hover
      -edge                     a darker edge around a fill, e.g. --o-focus-edge
      -tint                     a lighter tint as a surface, e.g. --o-immunity-proof-none-bg-tint
      -stop-{n}                 the stop n of a gradient, e.g. --o-highscore-podium-first-bg-stop-1
    A gradient stop can also turn the hue of its token (adjust-hue()), e.g.
    --o-catalog-header-noimage-stop-4.
    A translucent colour is no token. A rule mixes the token with transparent and keeps the
    alpha, e.g. color-mix(in srgb, var(--o-backdrop) 50%, transparent). An override of the token
    changes the translucent colour too. The defaults of the translucent $o-* variables (veils,
    tints of the primary colour) are such mixes, e.g. $o-login-bg-color; your value of the
    variable replaces it as before. For your own
    button rules use the mixin button-variant with colours as before, or with tokens:
      @include button-variant(var(--o-btn-primary-fg), var(--o-btn-primary-bg), var(--o-btn-primary-border));
    To override a token, set the map $o-tokens in your config. The key is the token name without
    "--o-". The value is the name of any other token ("primary", "tree-link"), a palette step
    "{hue}-{step}", a neutral step "neutral-{step}", "white" or a colour, e.g.
      $o-tokens: ("text-muted": "slate-600", "state-success-bg": "green-600", "state-success-fg": "white");
      $o-tokens: ("navbar-bg": "primary", "navbar-text": "primary-fg", "navbar-link": "primary-fg", "navbar-tools": "primary-fg", "bookmark": "red-600");
      $o-tokens: ("qti-menu-link": "link", "selectus-review-glow": "orange-500");
    The semantic tokens for surfaces, lines and veils read Bootstrap variables:
      --o-bg-hover       $table-bg-hover            row or item under the pointer
      --o-bg-selected    $table-bg-active           selected row or item
      --o-bg-raised      $dropdown-bg               dropdown menu, popover, dialog
      --o-border-subtle  $hr-border                 light separator line
      --o-text-disabled  $btn-link-disabled-color   text of a disabled control
      --o-backdrop       $modal-backdrop-bg         layer behind a modal dialog
      --o-shadow-color   black                      colour of the shadows
      --o-scrim          white                      light veil over content
    --o-shadow-color, --o-backdrop and --o-scrim are opaque. A rule sets the transparency, e.g.
      box-shadow: 0 4px 16px color-mix(in srgb, var(--o-shadow-color) 10%, transparent);
    The black shadows, the layer behind a dialog or a lightbox and the light veils of the modules
    read them this way.
    The Bootstrap colour variables that the modules read are framework tokens. Keep setting the
    Bootstrap variable, it gives the default of the token:
      --o-input-fg, --o-input-placeholder       $input-color, $input-color-placeholder
      --o-input-bg, --o-input-disabled-bg       reference "bg", "bg-subtle" (set with $o-tokens)
      --o-table-border, --o-table-striped-bg    $table-border-color, $table-bg-accent
      --o-table-hover-bg                        reference "bg-hover"
      --o-dropdown-link, -link-hover, -link-hover-bg, -divider   $dropdown-*
      --o-popover-border, --o-popover-bg        $popover-border-color, reference "bg-raised"
      --o-modal-content-border, --o-modal-header-border          $modal-*-border-color
      --o-code-block-bg, -fg, -border           $pre-bg, $pre-color, $pre-border-color
      --o-legend, --o-legend-border             $legend-color, $legend-border-color
      --o-panel-bg, --o-progress-bar-fg, --o-thumbnail-border, --o-blockquote-border
    The Bootstrap greys ($gray, $gray-light, $gray-dark, $gray-lighter) that a module reads are
    element tokens of that element, e.g. --o-alert-close, --o-chart-help-line, --o-qti-help.
    Links have four colour families. --o-link and --o-link-hover ($link-color, $link-hover-color)
    colour the standard links (a, .btn-link, pagination); an override of "link" reaches them.
    --o-link-subtle is the link colour adjusted to 4.5:1 on the subtle background: breadcrumb,
    table, course element and forum links read it ($o-table-link-color,
    $o-toolbar-breadcrumb-link-color, $o-forum-message-header-author-color and
    $o-forum-message-footer-attachment-color are removed, set the tokens with $o-tokens).
    --o-content-link ($o-content-link-color) colours the links in the content. --o-nav-link and
    --o-nav-link-hover ($navbar-default-link-color, $navbar-default-link-hover-color) colour the
    menu tree and toolbar links (--o-tree-link, --o-toolbar-link) when their Sass variables have
    the same colour. The navigation bar, the footer and the login help have own tokens: set
    them separately. These
    link tokens have the reference "link", set them with $o-tokens:
      --o-segments-tab-link            the filter tab row above a table: tab text, line of the
                                       selected tab, line under the row
      --o-selectus-review-comment-link the links in the review comments of selectus
    e.g.
      $o-tokens: ("segments-tab-link": "content-link");
    The status colours read the state tokens: the modules (icons, texts, tints, the danger
    button, the dirty button and the book button of the course site), the Bootstrap rules
    (.btn-success, .btn-info, .btn-warning, alerts, labels, progress bars, form validation)
    and the message boxes. They do not read $brand-success, $brand-info, $brand-warning,
    $brand-danger or $state-{success,info,warning,danger}-{text,bg,border} any more. Your
    values of these Bootstrap variables reach only email.css, the own rules of your theme and
    the login link of the navigation bar ($o-navbar-login-color, default $brand-warning).
    When your config sets one of them to a value other than the default of the light theme,
    the compile prints one warning for theme.css and one for content.css:
      WARNING: OpenOLAT theme: your config sets $brand-danger, ... These variables do not
      change the rules of theme.css and content.css any more ...
    The warning does not stop the compile. What to do:
      1. Set the colour of a state with $o-palette (the colour of a hue), $o-states (the hue
         of a state) or $o-tokens (one token), e.g.
           $o-tokens: ("state-danger-bg": "red-600", "state-danger-text": "red-600");
      2. Remove the Bootstrap variables from your config when your theme does not need them
         for email.css or for its own rules.
      3. When you keep them on purpose, set
           $o-legacy-color-warning: false;
         in your config to hide the warning.
    The danger button has own tokens with the danger state as default: btn-danger-bg,
    btn-danger-fg, btn-danger-border (one step darker). To change the danger button alone:
      $o-tokens: ("btn-danger-bg": "red-600", "btn-danger-border": "red-700");
    The dirty button ($o-button-dirty-bg) and the book button ($o-coursesite-book-bg) have the
    fill of the warning state and a dark text. When you set their Sass variable, the text is
    the adjusted white as before.
    The border of a styled course element is $o-cn-style-border-line-width (1px),
    $o-cn-style-border-line-style (solid) and the token course-style-border (#E4E7EC, follows
    "border"). The shorthand $o-cn-style-border is removed: when your config sets it, the
    compile prints one warning per stylesheet and the value has no effect. Set e.g.
      $o-cn-style-border-line-width: 2px;
      $o-tokens: ("course-style-border": "neutral-300");
    The text and the tool icons of the navigation bar ($o-navbar-color,
    $o-navbar-tools-permanent-color) default to $navbar-default-color adjusted to 4.5:1 on
    $o-navbar-bg. A value that your config sets is not adjusted.
    The focus ring: every control shows the same ring on :focus-visible, an outline of 2px in
    the colour of the token focus with a gap of 2px ($o-focus-ring-width, $o-focus-ring-offset).
    The token reads $o-focus-color. Default: $brand-primary adjusted to 3:1 on $body-bg. When
    your config sets $input-border-focus, the ring has that colour. A value that your config
    sets for $o-focus-color or $input-border-focus is not adjusted: check 3:1 on the page
    background yourself. The navigation bar and the footer have own tokens (navbar-focus,
    footer-focus): the focus colour when it has 3:1 on the navigation bar or the footer, else
    their link colour, adjusted to 3:1. On a coloured navigation bar you can set e.g.
      $o-tokens: ("navbar-focus": "white");
    Do not add own outline rules for :focus to your theme; the mixins of light/modules/_focus.scss
    give a control the inset ring (o-focus-ring-inset) when it touches its neighbours.
    The fixed greys and colours of the modules read tokens (decision D29): texts, borders and
    fills read the semantic tokens (text, text-muted, border, border-strong, bg-subtle, bg-hover,
    ...), status icons the state tokens, decorative colours palette steps. Your values of
    $text-color, $panel-default-border, $input-border, ... now reach these rules too. Elements
    without a fitting role have own tokens with a scale step: the one-time code field
    (otp-cell-bg, otp-cell-active-bg, otp-caret), the off-canvas menu (offcanvas-link-hover,
    which reads $o-offcanvas-link-hover-color, offcanvas-category-border), the status icons of
    selectus (selectus-status-*), the audio and video recorder (recorder-*), the sticky table
    columns (table-sticky-col-bg, table-sticky-col-striped-bg), e.g.
      $o-tokens: ("otp-cell-bg": "neutral-100", "selectus-status-screening": "yellow-600");
    The QTI hotspot editor styles, the styles stored in content (image styles, text markers),
    brand colours, the developer tools and the print sheets keep their literal colours.
    The framework modules (dialogs and message boxes, login page, cards and widgets, navigation
    bar and offcanvas menu, table, menu tree, icons) read element tokens fed by their Sass
    variables, e.g. $o-box-tip-color gives --o-box-tip-border, $o-login-lead-color gives
    --o-login-lead, $o-tree-border-color gives --o-tree-border, $o-im-status-available-color
    gives --o-im-status-available. Keep setting the Sass variables, or set the token with
    $o-tokens. Still Sass: $o-tree-bg, $o-tree-link-bg, $o-tree-link-active-bg and
    $o-navbar-border-color (they accept "none").
    The tests, the page editor, the practice and the video modules read element tokens fed by
    their Sass variables too, e.g. $o-qti-menu-border-color gives --o-qti-menu-border,
    $o-editor-border-color gives --o-editor-border, $o-practice-progress-l1-bg-color gives
    --o-practice-progress-l1-bg, $o-video-controls-bg gives --o-video-controls-bg.
    A Sass variable with a translucent default feeds its token with that value:
    $o-login-bg-color (--o-login-bg), $o-login-infomessage-bg, $o-imagebg-text-background,
    $o-fact-icon-bg-color, $o-drawing-select-bg, $o-practice-play-main-bg-color,
    $o-video-startbox-main-bg-color. Set the variable to a colour or set the token, e.g.
      $o-tokens: ("login-bg": "white");
    The shorthand border variables are removed. A border is a width, a style and a colour
    token: $o-card-border-width, $o-card-border-style and the token card-border. The same
    for widget, fact-sheet, fact-sheet-separator, dashboard-widget, table-details-container,
    activity-add, activity-modify, activity-remove, repo-info, repo-info-separator and
    editor-layout-inner-h, -v (the token name has no "o-"), e.g.
      $o-card-border-width: 2px;
      $o-tokens: ("card-border": "neutral-300");
    When your config sets a removed shorthand ($o-card-border, ...), the compile prints one
    warning per stylesheet and the value has no effect. $o-login-border stays valid: when
    your config sets it, the login box uses the value as it is and the compile prints a
    warning. Prefer $o-login-border-width, $o-login-border-style and $o-login-border-color.
    The course, curriculum, user, chat, high score, projects, radial progress, Selectus and
    various modules read element tokens fed by their Sass variables too, e.g.
    $o-edu-type-exam-course-color gives --o-edu-type-exam-course, $o-im-chat-message-bg-me
    gives --o-im-chat-message-me-bg, $o-radial-progress-circle-color gives
    --o-radial-progress-circle, $o-curriculum-import-bg-error gives
    --o-curriculum-import-error-bg. More translucent defaults feed their token:
    $o-brand-box-bg, $o-cn-style-cover-text-bg (--o-course-style-cover-text-bg), $o-lp-bg,
    $o-cat2-header-box-bg-color (--o-catalog-header-box-bg), ... More shorthand borders are
    removed: icon-panel, lp, curriculum-widget, curriculum-widget-card, indicator-separator,
    user-info, user-info-profile, user-info-divider, user-info-portrait,
    panel-authentication ($o-icon-panel-border-width, ..., token icon-panel-border).
    Four removed shorthands have other names: $o-empty-border-color ($o-empty-border-width,
    -style, token empty-border), $o-assessment-inspection-border-color
    ($o-assessment-inspection-border-width, -style, token assessment-inspection-border),
    $o-hs-podium-border ($o-hs-podium-border-width, -style, token highscore-podium-border)
    and $o-users-portrait-img-border ($o-user-info-portrait-border-width, -style, token
    user-info-portrait-border). The compile prints one warning per stylesheet when your
    config sets one of them.
    The other modules read element tokens fed by their Sass variables too (OO-10043 slice
    19), e.g. $o-tag-font-color gives --o-tag-fg, $o-competences-font-color gives
    --o-competence-fg, $o-toolbar-nav-bg-color gives --o-toolbar-nav-bg,
    $o-qti-textEntryInteraction-bg gives --o-qti-text-entry-bg. Eight more shorthand
    borders are removed: $o-folder-row-thumbnail-image-border and $o-swiper-button-border
    ($o-swiper-button-border-width, -style, token swiper-button-border), $o-step-border
    ($o-step-border-line-width, -style, token step-border), $o-ac-ba-border
    ($o-ac-ba-border-width, -style, token ac-billing-address-border) and the topic broker
    borders $o-tb-details-separator-border-bottom, $o-tb-topic-card-title-abbr-border,
    $o-tb-topic-placeholder-title-abbr-border, $o-tb-weighting-grid-border (-width,
    -style, tokens topicbroker-details-separator-border, topicbroker-title-abbr-border,
    topicbroker-placeholder-title-abbr-border, topicbroker-weighting-grid-border).
    High score: $o-hs-position-title-gradient: false; shows the position title in the
    colour of the token highscore-position-title (default: the text colour). The old
    $o-hs-position-title-color still works ("gradient" or a colour) and the compile
    prints a warning.
    The compile stops for an unknown token or step, for a loop of references and for an override
    below the contrast of its pair (4.5:1 text, 3:1 borders and focus). An override changes every
    rule that reads the token; a rule that still reads the Sass variable keeps the Sass value. Do
    not add new Sass colour variables to your theme for new components; a new customization point
    is a token.
    Details: doc/openolat-frontend.md, chapter 26.
  - when the client insists on colours with too little contrast: set
      $o-contrast-check: "warn";
    in your config. The compile then reports each colour pair below its contrast ratio
    (palette, theme colours, $o-tokens) as a warning and accepts it. The default "error" stops
    the compile. With "warn" the theme does not meet WCAG 2.1 AA; set it only on the explicit
    wish of the client. The light and openolat themes always use "error".
- Open mytheme/_mytheme_theme.scss and override or extend styles from the light theme. Best is to use the 
  browser to lookup the CSS classes and then search in the light theme for the definitions. Don't miss the 
  tag hierarchies and the responsive rules!
- Open mytheme/_mytheme_content.scss and override or extend styles from the light theme. 

Optional:
- Place your own favicon.ico file to mytheme/favicon.ico (legacy support)
- Place your own favicon (new style) and app icons to 
  mytheme/meta/favicon.16.png  				-> optional, to add a 16x16 bookmark icon
  mytheme/meta/favicon.32.png  				-> optional, to add a 32x32 bookmark icon
  mytheme/meta/favicon.64.png  				-> optional, to add a 64x64 bookmark icon
- Place your own application icons for older iOS to
  mytheme/meta/appicon180.png  				-> optional, to add iOS homescreen icons
- Place your own manifest and application icons for modern Safari and Android to
  mytheme/meta/manifest.json  				-> optional, to add Android homescreen icons (and add the appicons linked from within manifest.json)
- Place your own Windows Tile icons to
  mytheme/meta/tileicon70png
  mytheme/meta/tileicon150png
  mytheme/meta/tileicon310png
- OR alternatively add a browser config file to the location below. 
  mytheme/meta/msapplication-config.xml		-> optional, to add Windows tiles icons 
  Note, when the browser config is there, the tile icons above will not be included. In the browser config the 
  links to the tile icons must be absolute path to work despite the MS doku tells otherwise

- Create a file mytheme/theme.js and add your theme specific JavaScript. Best is to create a JQuery module 
  for it. Make sure you check for necessary changes after each DOM replacement cycle. See the theme.js file
  in the openolat theme for an example.


Compiling a theme
=======================
Compile your theme using the simple script:

cd openolat/src/main/webapp/static/themes/
./compiletheme.sh mytheme

If you don't have SASS installed, see "Installing SASS below"

Testing a custom theme
======================
Once you have created the new theme you can log in as administrator and select the 
Administration site in the main navigation. Choose 'Customizing' -> 'Layout' from the menu. 
You new theme will appear in the themes list. Select your theme to apply it. 

Note that the theme will be applied to the whole OpenOLAT installation. All users that log into
the system will work with the selected right away.

Don't forget to tweet @openolat to report your success together with a screenshot of your new theme!


Install SASS
======================
OpenOlat requires Dart Sass 1.79.0 or later (the theme colours use the colour space functions).

Install on macOS with brew:
brew install sass/sass/sass

Install on Windows with choco:
choco install sass

For more information, visit https://sass-lang.com/install.


